module

public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.CompactObjects
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.LocalizingAdmissible

/-!
# The Neeman–Thomason description of the compact objects of a localizing subcategory

Let `D` be a triangulated category with direct sums indexed by its morphism universe `v`, and let
`G : ι → D` be a family of *compact* objects indexed by a nonempty type `ι : Type v`. Write

`N = ⟨G⟩_loc` for the localizing (coproduct-closed, triangulated, retract-closed) subcategory
generated by the family, and `⟨G⟩ = ObjectProperty.triangEnvelope` for its *thick* closure — the
smallest retract-closed triangulated subcategory containing the family.

This file proves

`N ⊓ D_c = ⟨G⟩`,

that is: an object of `N` is compact in `D` exactly when it is built from the `G i` by shifts,
finite direct sums, extensions and direct summands, with no infinite direct sums needed. The
inclusion `⟨G⟩ ≤ N ⊓ D_c` is formal; the content is the other one, and it is the theorem of
Neeman and Thomason.

## The argument

The proof is [Stacks, Tag 09SR] applied *inside* `N`, and the two things to supply are the
transfer of compactness and the transfer of the envelope across the inclusion
`N.ι : N.FullSubcategory ⥤ D`.

* *Compactness, from `D` to `N`.* For an object `X` of a full subcategory closed under the direct
  sums of `D`, compactness of `X.obj` in `D` gives compactness of `X` in the subcategory: the
  inclusion is full, faithful, additive, and preserves those direct sums, so
  `CategoryTheory.isCompactObject_of_fullyFaithful` applies. This is
  `isCompactObject_fullSubcategory`. The converse — compact in `N` implies compact in `D` — is
  *not* proved and *not* needed: the direction consumed by the argument is the one that turns the
  hypothesis "compact in `D`" into the hypothesis of [Stacks, Tag 09SR] inside `N`, and the same
  transfer makes the generators `G i`, read inside `N`, a family of compact objects there.
* *Generation inside `N`.* The family `G` read inside `N` generates `N`. This is
  `ObjectProperty.isZero_of_rightOrthogonal_shiftClosure_localizingClosureGen`, already proved for
  Brown representability in
  `StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/Triangulated/LocalizingAdmissible.lean`.
* *[Stacks, Tag 09SR] inside `N`.* `N.FullSubcategory` is triangulated and has the direct sums
  indexed by `Type v` that `N` is closed under, and the family of generators read there is compact
  and generating. So its triangulated envelope, formed inside `N.FullSubcategory`, is exactly the
  property of being a compact object of `N.FullSubcategory`, by
  `triangEnvelope_eq_isCompactObject_of_isWeakTriangulatedGenerator_ofObj`.
* *The envelope, from `N` back to `D`.* The preimage along `N.ι` of `⟨G⟩` is retract-closed and
  triangulated (`inverseImage_isStableUnderRetracts`, `isTriangulated_inverseImage`: the inclusion
  is fully faithful, exact, and commutes with the shift) and contains the generators read inside
  `N`, so by the minimality of the envelope it contains the envelope formed inside `N`. Pushing an
  object of the latter forward therefore lands in `⟨G⟩`.

Nothing in the argument uses Brown representability or the right admissibility of `N`; the import
of `LocalizingAdmissible.lean` is only for the generation lemma and for
`ObjectProperty.localizingClosureGen`.

## Where this is used

[Stacks, Tag 09LK] identifies the target of the localization of `K(C)` at `Z = Φ⁻¹ N` with the
derived category of an abelian category, and that identification needs `Z` to be the acyclic
complexes. The inclusion `Z ≤ Ac` is exactly where the present theorem enters: `h_X` is compact,
so if it lies in `N` it lies in the *thick* closure of the `h_{G i}`, which is a statement that
can be transported along the fully faithful Yoneda functor, whereas membership in the localizing
closure cannot. See
`StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/DifferentialGraded/RightModule/YonedaKernelAcyclic.lean`.

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.isCompactObject_fullSubcategory`.
* `CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.inverseImage_isStableUnderRetracts` and
  `CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.isTriangulated_inverseImage`.
* `CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.localizingClosure_inf_isCompactObject_eq_triangEnvelope`: the
  theorem of Neeman and Thomason.

## References

* [Stacks project, Tag 09SR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09SR)
* [Neeman, *The connection between the K-theory localization theorem of Thomason, Trobaugh and
  Yao and the smashing subcategories of Bousfield and Ravenel*][neeman1992]
-/

@[expose] public section

universe v' u' v u

namespace CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty

open Category Limits Opposite Preadditive Pretriangulated ZeroObject

/-! ### Compactness inside a full subcategory -/

section FullSubcategory

variable {D : Type u} [Category.{v} D] [Preadditive D] [HasCoproducts.{v} D]

/-- **Compactness passes from the ambient category to a full subcategory** closed under the
direct sums indexed by the morphism universe: the inclusion is full, faithful, additive and
preserves those direct sums, so compactness descends along it
(`CategoryTheory.isCompactObject_of_fullyFaithful`).

Only this direction is true in general. An object compact in the subcategory need not be compact
in `D`, because a direct sum taken in `D` of objects of the subcategory need not lie in it. -/
lemma isCompactObject_fullSubcategory (P : ObjectProperty D)
    [∀ J : Type v, P.IsClosedUnderColimitsOfShape (Discrete J)] (X : P.FullSubcategory)
    (hX : IsCompactObject.{v} X.obj) : IsCompactObject.{v} X := by
  have : ∀ J : Type v, PreservesColimitsOfShape (Discrete J) P.ι := fun _ ↦ inferInstance
  exact isCompactObject_of_fullyFaithful P.ι X hX

end FullSubcategory

/-! ### The preimage of a thick subcategory along an exact functor -/

section InverseImage

variable {D : Type u} [Category.{v} D] [Preadditive D] [HasZeroObject D] [HasShift D ℤ]
  [∀ n : ℤ, (shiftFunctor D n).Additive] [Pretriangulated D]
  {E : Type u'} [Category.{v'} E] [Preadditive E] [HasZeroObject E] [HasShift E ℤ]
  [∀ n : ℤ, (shiftFunctor E n).Additive] [Pretriangulated E]
  (P : ObjectProperty D) (F : E ⥤ D)

omit [Preadditive D] [HasZeroObject D] [HasShift D ℤ] [∀ n : ℤ, (shiftFunctor D n).Additive]
  [Pretriangulated D] [Preadditive E] [HasZeroObject E] [HasShift E ℤ]
  [∀ n : ℤ, (shiftFunctor E n).Additive] [Pretriangulated E] in
/-- The preimage of a retract-closed property of objects is retract-closed: a functor carries a
retract to a retract. -/
lemma inverseImage_isStableUnderRetracts [P.IsStableUnderRetracts] :
    (P.inverseImage F).IsStableUnderRetracts where
  of_retract r hY := P.prop_of_retract (r.map F) hY

/-- The preimage of a triangulated subcategory along an exact functor commuting with the shift is
a triangulated subcategory: the zero object goes to a zero object, the preimage is shift-stable
because `F` commutes with the shift, and an extension in `E` is carried to an extension in `D`. -/
lemma isTriangulated_inverseImage [P.IsClosedUnderIsomorphisms] [P.IsTriangulated]
    [F.CommShift ℤ] [F.IsTriangulated] : (P.inverseImage F).IsTriangulated := by
  have hext : (P.inverseImage F).IsTriangulatedClosed₂ :=
    IsTriangulatedClosed₂.mk' fun T hT h₁ h₃ ↦
      P.ext_of_isTriangulatedClosed₂ (F.mapTriangle.obj T) (F.map_distinguished T hT) h₁ h₃
  exact ⟨⟩

end InverseImage

/-! ### The theorem of Neeman and Thomason -/

section NeemanThomason

variable {D : Type u} [Category.{v} D] [Preadditive D] [HasZeroObject D] [HasShift D ℤ]
  [∀ n : ℤ, (shiftFunctor D n).Additive] [Pretriangulated D] [IsTriangulated D]
  [HasCoproducts.{v} D] {ι : Type v} (G : ι → D) [∀ i, IsCompactObject.{v} (G i)]

omit [IsTriangulated D] in
/-- A generator, read as an object of the localizing subcategory it helps generate, is compact
*there* as soon as it is compact in the ambient category: the subcategory is closed under the
direct sums of the ambient category. -/
lemma isCompactObject_localizingClosureGen (i : ι) :
    IsCompactObject.{v} (localizingClosureGen G i) :=
  isCompactObject_fullSubcategory _ _ (show IsCompactObject.{v} (G i) from inferInstance)

omit [HasCoproducts.{v} D] [∀ i, IsCompactObject.{v} (G i)] in
/-- The envelope of the generators read inside `N` is carried by the inclusion into the envelope
of the generators in `D`: the preimage of the latter is a retract-closed triangulated subcategory
of `N.FullSubcategory` containing the generators, so the minimality of the envelope applies. -/
lemma triangEnvelope_obj_of_triangEnvelope_localizingClosureGen [Nonempty ι]
    {X : (ofObj G).localizingClosure.{v}.FullSubcategory}
    (hX : (ofObj (localizingClosureGen G)).triangEnvelope X) :
    (ofObj G).triangEnvelope X.obj := by
  have := inverseImage_isStableUnderRetracts (ofObj G).triangEnvelope
    (ofObj G).localizingClosure.{v}.ι
  have := isTriangulated_inverseImage (ofObj G).triangEnvelope
    (ofObj G).localizingClosure.{v}.ι
  have hle : (ofObj (localizingClosureGen G)).triangEnvelope ≤
      (ofObj G).triangEnvelope.inverseImage (ofObj G).localizingClosure.{v}.ι := by
    rw [triangEnvelope_le_iff, ofObj_le_iff]
    exact fun i ↦ le_triangEnvelope _ _ (ofObj_apply G i)
  exact hle X hX

/-- **The theorem of Neeman and Thomason.** Let `D` be a triangulated category with direct sums
indexed by its morphism universe, and let `G : ι → D` be a nonempty family of compact objects
indexed by a type of that universe. Then the compact objects of `D` lying in the localizing
subcategory `N` generated by the family are exactly the objects of its *thick* closure:

`N ⊓ D_c = ⟨G⟩`.

The inclusion `⟨G⟩ ≤ N ⊓ D_c` is formal — the compact objects form a retract-closed triangulated
subcategory containing the family ([Stacks, Tag 09QH]), and so does `N`. The content is the
reverse inclusion, and it is [Stacks, Tag 09SR] applied inside `N`: the family read inside `N` is
a family of compact generators of `N`, so the triangulated envelope formed there is the whole
subcategory of compact objects of `N`, and an object of `N` compact in `D` is compact in `N`. -/
theorem localizingClosure_inf_isCompactObject_eq_triangEnvelope [Nonempty ι] :
    (ofObj G).localizingClosure.{v} ⊓ IsCompactObject.{v, _, _} (C := D) =
      (ofObj G).triangEnvelope := by
  obtain ⟨hiso, htri, -, -⟩ :
      IsClosedUnderIsomorphisms (IsCompactObject.{v, _, _} (C := D)) ∧
        ObjectProperty.IsTriangulated (IsCompactObject.{v, _, _} (C := D)) ∧
        IsSaturated (IsCompactObject.{v, _, _} (C := D)) ∧
        IsIdempotentComplete (FullSubcategory (IsCompactObject.{v, _, _} (C := D))) :=
    isClosedUnderIsomorphisms_isTriangulated_isSaturated_and_isIdempotentComplete_isCompactObject
  have hretr : IsStableUnderRetracts (IsCompactObject.{v, _, _} (C := D)) :=
    ⟨fun r hB ↦ isCompactObject_of_retract hB r.i r.r r.retract⟩
  refine le_antisymm (fun X hX ↦ ?_) (le_inf ?_ ?_)
  · -- `X` lies in `N` and is compact in `D`, hence compact in `N`.
    have : ∀ i, IsCompactObject.{v} (localizingClosureGen G i) :=
      isCompactObject_localizingClosureGen G
    have : ∀ (i : ι) (α : Type v),
        PreservesColimitsOfShape (Discrete α) (preadditiveCoyoneda.obj (op (G i))) :=
      fun i α ↦ preservesColimitsOfShape_discrete_of_isCompactObject (G i) α
    refine triangEnvelope_obj_of_triangEnvelope_localizingClosureGen G (X := ⟨X, hX.1⟩) ?_
    -- [Stacks, Tag 09SR] inside `N`.
    rw [triangEnvelope_eq_isCompactObject_of_isWeakTriangulatedGenerator_ofObj
      (localizingClosureGen G) (isZero_of_rightOrthogonal_shiftClosure_localizingClosureGen G)]
    exact isCompactObject_fullSubcategory _ _ hX.2
  · exact triangEnvelope_le_localizingClosure _
  · rw [triangEnvelope_le_iff, ofObj_le_iff]
    exact fun i ↦ inferInstance

end NeemanThomason

end CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty

end
